---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { url } from '../data/site';
import { releases } from '../data/releases';

const sections = [
  ['getting-started', 'Start here'],
  ['installation', 'Install a world or the kit'],
  ['first-map', 'Build your first map'],
  ['concepts', 'How map setup works'],
  ['gameplay', 'Start a game'],
  ['hud', 'HUD and player setup'],
  ['advanced', 'Keep building'],
  ['troubleshooting', 'Troubleshooting'],
];
---

<Layout
  title="Tutorials | Zombies Build Kit"
  description="Install Zombies Build Kit and learn the basics of building and running a custom Zombies map."
  theme="build"
>
  <section class="page-intro wrap">
    <p class="eyebrow">FIELD GUIDE / ZBK</p>
    <h1>BUILD WITH <em>ZBK.</em></h1>
    <p class="lede">Everything you need to turn a Minecraft world into a playable Zombies map.</p>
  </section>

  <div class="docs-layout wrap">
    <aside class="docs-nav" aria-label="Tutorial sections">
      <p class="eyebrow">IN THIS GUIDE</p>
      <nav>
        {sections.map(([id, label]) => <a href={`#${id}`}>{label}</a>)}
      </nav>
      <a class="docs-download" href={url('downloads/')}>Get the downloads <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
    </aside>

    <article class="guide">
      <section id="getting-started">
        <p class="eyebrow">01 / BEFORE YOU BEGIN</p>
        <h2>Choose the right build</h2>
        <p>Zombies Build Kit runs on <strong>Minecraft Java Edition 26.2</strong>. Choose a bundled world to start building, or install the standalone datapack and matching resource pack in your own world.</p>
        <p>You need a world where you can use commands and edit map markers. In multiplayer, make sure you have permission to use the Build Kit dialogs and commands.</p>
        <div class="guide-callout"><strong>Everything a map needs, in one download.</strong> Map and starter world archives include their datapacks and resource pack. Structures are bundled in the datapack.</div>
      </section>

      <section id="installation">
        <p class="eyebrow">02 / INSTALL</p>
        <h2>Install a world or the kit</h2>
        <h3>Play a map or use the starter world</h3>
        <ol>
          <li>Choose a world archive from the <a href={releases.maps} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">map releases</a>. Use the packaged world asset, not GitHub's source-code archive.</li>
          <li>Close Minecraft and extract the world folder into your launcher's <code>saves</code> folder. The folder should contain <code>level.dat</code> directly.</li>
          <li>Keep the included datapacks, resource pack, and other world files together. Open the world in Minecraft Java {releases.minecraft} and follow its release notes.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>The resource pack is inside the world download. You do not need to download another resource pack or a separate structures package for these worlds. Back up an existing world before replacing or updating it.</p>
        <h3>Add ZBK to your own world</h3>
        <ol>
          <li>Create your world first, then close Minecraft and open its folder inside <code>saves</code>.</li>
          <li>Get the base Zombies Build Kit datapack from the <a href={releases.datapack} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">datapack releases</a>. Put its ZIP or extracted <code>zombies_build_kit</code> folder in the world's <code>datapacks</code> folder, with <code>pack.mcmeta</code> at the pack root.</li>
          <li>Get the matching base pack from the <a href={releases.resourcePack} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">resource pack releases</a>. Put its ZIP or folder in your launcher's <code>resourcepacks</code> folder and enable it under <strong>Options → Resource Packs</strong>.</li>
          <li><strong>Optional: add your own datapack code.</strong> To extend ZBK with custom gameplay, install your own map add-on alongside the base datapack, or use <code>zbk_template</code> as a starting point. Enable only one map provider; replace the template when your add-on is ready.</li>
          <li>Open the world and run <code>/reload</code>. Check <code>/datapack list</code> to confirm the packs are enabled.</li>
        </ol>
        <div class="guide-callout"><strong>Structures are already included.</strong> The base datapack supplies the shared structure templates. No separate structures download or manual structures-folder installation is needed.</div>
      </section>

      <section id="first-map">
        <p class="eyebrow">03 / FIRST SESSION</p>
        <h2>Open the builder</h2>
        <p>Open the Zombies hub from the pause menu with <kbd>Esc</kbd> → <strong>Zombies</strong>, or use <strong>Quick Actions</strong> (<kbd>G</kbd>). You can also use this command with cheats or command permission enabled:</p>
        <div class="code-line"><code>/dialog open @s zombies:zombies</code><button class="copy-button" data-copy="/dialog open @s zombies:zombies">Copy</button></div>
        <p>Choose <strong>Build Kit</strong> in the dialog to get the Build Manager stick. The Build Kit is available while a game is not active. You can also open it from the in-world Spawn Menu when a map has one.</p>
        <ol>
          <li>Open <strong>Build Kit → Map Elements</strong> and choose a marker type, such as Spawn.</li>
          <li>Place the marker item where you want that feature in the world.</li>
          <li>Hold the Build Manager to reveal nearby markers. Right-click near a marker with it to open that marker’s settings.</li>
          <li>Set up player spawns, a worldspawn, a Spawn Menu, and at least one zombie spawner. Add doors and more rooms as your map grows.</li>
        </ol>
        <div class="flow-row" aria-label="Map building flow"><span>Choose marker</span><b aria-hidden="true">→</b><span>Place in world</span><b aria-hidden="true">→</b><span>Configure</span><b aria-hidden="true">→</b><span>Test</span></div>
      </section>

      <section id="concepts">
        <p class="eyebrow">04 / MAP LOGIC</p>
        <h2>Markers, links, and zones</h2>
        <p><strong>Markers save your map setup.</strong> Runtime displays and interactions are rebuilt from the marker configuration when the datapack initializes. Configure the markers in the world instead of treating their visible models as the saved settings.</p>
        <p><strong>Zones control where enemies spawn.</strong> Zone 0 is active when a game begins. Zones 1–32 remain locked until a door unlocks them. Give each zombie or dog spawner a zone, then add the zones that a door should unlock. A connecting door may need to unlock both neighboring zones so players can approach it from either side.</p>
        <p><strong>Linked pieces need matching IDs.</strong> Some features use several markers as one system. For a jump pad, place Start, Peak, and End markers and give all three the same Link ID. Use the marker dialog’s used-ID option where available to avoid collisions.</p>
        <div class="guide-callout"><strong>Build in a loop.</strong> Place a small set of markers, configure them, start a test game, then adjust. Zone Highlight in Build Kit → Zombie Control helps check which markers belong to a zone.</div>
      </section>

      <section id="gameplay">
        <p class="eyebrow">05 / PLAYTEST</p>
        <h2>Run a round</h2>
        <p>Before starting, check that player spawn markers exist, the world has a sensible return point, and at least one zombie spawner belongs to active Zone 0. Use the in-world Spawn Menu or the Zombies dialog to start the game. Build Kit editing is intended for outside an active game.</p>
        <p>Walk the route a player will take. Try opening each door and confirm the expected zones activate. Check that spawners are reachable, players have room to move, and the game can be stopped and started again cleanly.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="hud">
        <p class="eyebrow">06 / PLAYER PRESENTATION</p>
        <h2>Check the HUD and resource pack</h2>
        <p>If the game runs but custom models, weapon art, sounds, fonts, or HUD icons are missing, enable the matching Zombies Build Kit resource pack. After changing local resource-pack assets, press <kbd>F3</kbd>+<kbd>T</kbd> to reload them.</p>
        <p>For Vivecraft, follow the dedicated setup instructions for the release you installed. The VR overlay and companion mod are optional; they are not required for the standard Java setup.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="advanced">
        <p class="eyebrow">07 / NEXT BUILD</p>
        <h2>Make the map your own</h2>
        <p>Add doors, barriers, wall guns, perks, power, traps, jump pads, and Mystery Box locations as your layout needs them. Give each feature a clear purpose and test its settings in the world. Map / Sound ID selects the map-specific sound set; custom sound packs use their documented ID and namespace rules.</p>
        <p>Browse the <a href={url('#build')}>Build Kit guide</a> for builder details, or return to <a href={url('downloads/')}>Downloads</a> to choose a package.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="troubleshooting">
        <p class="eyebrow">08 / QUICK FIXES</p>
        <h2>When something does not load</h2>
        <details>
          <summary>The datapack does not appear in <code>/datapack list</code>.</summary>
          <p>Check that the extracted datapack folder is directly inside this world’s <code>datapacks</code> directory and that <code>pack.mcmeta</code> is directly inside the datapack folder. Run <code>/reload</code> again.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>The game runs, but custom art or sounds are missing.</summary>
          <p>Enable the resource pack that matches the installed datapack and the Minecraft version named in its release notes. Reload resources with <kbd>F3</kbd>+<kbd>T</kbd> after local asset edits.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>I cannot find or configure a marker.</summary>
          <p>Use the Build Manager outside an active game. Hold it to reveal markers within about 15 blocks, then right-click within about 5 blocks of the marker to open its settings.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>No enemies appear in a room.</summary>
          <p>Check that the zombie or dog spawner has the intended zone and that the zone is active. Zone 0 starts active; other zones need to be unlocked by a door.</p>
        </details>
      </section>
      <aside class="guide-callout" aria-labelledby="more-help">
        <h2 id="more-help">Still need a hand?</h2>
        <p>Ask about setup, troubleshoot your map, or get help from the ZBK community.</p>
        <a class="text-link" href="https://discord.gg/CjsmDXtznD" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Get help on Discord <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
      </aside>
    </article>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .docs-layout { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 13rem minmax(0, 1fr); gap: clamp(2rem, 6vw, 6rem); padding-bottom: 7rem; }
  .docs-nav { align-self: start; position: sticky; top: 2rem; display: grid; gap: 1rem; }
  .docs-nav nav { display: grid; gap: .7rem; }
  .docs-nav a, .guide a { color: var(--accent, #e0a748); text-underline-offset: .2em; }
  .docs-nav nav a { color: inherit; text-decoration: none; opacity: .72; }
  .docs-nav nav a:hover { opacity: 1; }
  .docs-download { margin-top: .7rem; font-weight: 700; }
  .guide { min-width: 0; max-width: 52rem; }
  .guide section { scroll-margin-top: 2rem; padding: 0 0 3rem; margin: 0 0 3rem; border-bottom: 1px solid rgb(255 255 255 / 12%); }
  .guide h2 { margin: .35rem 0 1rem; font-size: clamp(1.8rem, 4vw, 2.7rem); line-height: 1.08; }
  .guide p, .guide li { line-height: 1.75; }
  .guide ol { padding-left: 1.35rem; }
  .guide li { padding-left: .25rem; margin: .45rem 0; }
  .guide code { padding: .12em .35em; border-radius: .25rem; background: rgb(255 255 255 / 9%); font-size: .92em; }
  .guide .code-line code { background: none; padding: 0; }
  .guide-callout { margin: 1.4rem 0; padding: 1rem 1.2rem; border-left: 3px solid var(--accent, #e0a748); background: rgb(255 255 255 / 5%); line-height: 1.65; }
  .code-line { display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between; gap: 1rem; padding: .85rem 1rem; margin: 1rem 0; border: 1px solid rgb(255 255 255 / 15%); border-radius: .4rem; background: rgb(0 0 0 / 30%); }
  .copy-button { cursor: pointer; padding: .4rem .7rem; color: inherit; border: 1px solid rgb(255 255 255 / 25%); border-radius: .25rem; background: transparent; }
  .flow-row { display: flex; flex-direction: column; align-items: flex-start; gap: .2rem; margin: 1.5rem 0; counter-reset: flow-step; }
  .flow-row span { display: flex; align-items: baseline; gap: .85rem; padding: .25rem 0; counter-increment: flow-step; }
  .flow-row span::before { content: counter(flow-step, decimal-leading-zero); color: var(--accent); font-size: .75rem; letter-spacing: .08em; font-weight: 600; }
  .flow-row b { color: var(--accent); width: 1.25rem; line-height: 1.25rem; text-align: center; transform: rotate(90deg); font-weight: 400; }
  .guide details { padding: 1rem 0; border-top: 1px solid rgb(255 255 255 / 12%); }
  .guide summary { cursor: pointer; font-weight: 700; }
  .guide details p { margin-bottom: 0; }
  @media (max-width: 760px) {
    .docs-layout { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 2.5rem; }
    .docs-nav { position: static; grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }
    .docs-nav nav { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); }
  }
</style>
